  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Trans women without borders against transphobia and HIV/AIDS - Americas - Multicountry Americas REDLACTRANS (01-Apr-2016-31-Mar-2019)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Transgender people in the Latin America and the Caribbean (LAC) region are disproportionately affected by HIV and AIDS as compared to other key populations.  They have a HIV prevalence rate of between 26 and 35 percent, and a life expectancy between 35.5 and 41.25 years, with HIV and AIDS as the main cause of death.  To date, HIV and AIDS National Strategic Plans in the LAC Region address prevention, treatment and care including rapid diagnosis, treatment scale-up, monitoring and evaluation of interventions, among others.  Among the results identified include the expansion of integrated treatment and care for people living with HIV, increased national investment in HIV (particularly in El Salvador, Paraguay, Ecuador and Honduras), improvements of quality of healthcare in Central America, integration of key populations among the target populations and the provision of differentiated services, such as transgender friendly clinics.  This program aims to cover identified gaps not currently supported by regional efforts or country-level activities in order to reduce human rights barriers, and enhance the impact of current HIV national responses towards transgender people in the region.  This will be achieved by promoting a positive legal environment with respect to the human rights of transgender people in LAC, and to contribute to better access to comprehensive healthcare in the HIV and AIDS response.</narrative>
  </description>
